---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SubscribeForm from '../components/SubscribeForm.astro';
import { CADENCE_LINE } from '../lib/site';

// THE SUBSCRIBE DOOR (editors' desktop review, 2026-09-07).
//
// WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. The email field stood in the footer of every page on the
// site, one element among four rows of links, and the editors replaced it there
// with a link reading SUBSCRIBE TO THE JOURNAL. The instruction was explicit
// that the form move rather than go: a journal that stops offering the door
// while keeping the sign for it has done the one thing this change was not for.
// So the field is here, and it is the whole of the page — a reader who presses
// that link arrives at the thing they pressed it for, above the fold at every
// width, with nothing to scroll past to reach it.
//
// THE CADENCE COMES FROM CADENCE_LINE, never from a sentence typed here. It is
// the journal's own present-tense statement of how often it publishes (R-039,
// R-055), it renders on the masthead of every issue and in the digest, and it
// moves when cadence moves. A page that told a prospective subscriber how often
// mail would arrive, in its own words, would be the one surface that went stale
// the day the cadence changed.
//
// NO PROMISES UNDER THE FIELD. "No tracking, unsubscribe anytime." was retired
// on 2026-09-02 — the practice unchanged, the claim moved to the terms, where it
// binds. This page does not reinstate it in different words, in a new place,
// three weeks later; it links to the terms instead, which is where a reader can
// hold us to something. The receipt a reader sees after pressing Subscribe still
// names the unsubscribe link, because at that point it is a fact about the mail
// they are about to get rather than reassurance offered to win the press.
//
// A SIGNUP IS A SUBSCRIPTION, since 2026-08-22 — there is no confirmation step
// to describe, and this page must not invent one. See src/lib/subscribe-copy.mjs,
// which holds the words for what actually happens next.
---

<Base
  title="Subscribe"
  description="Each issue of The Latent Review, delivered by email when it publishes."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-header">
      <p class="kicker kicker--accent kicker--lead">Subscribe</p>
      <h1>Each issue, when it publishes</h1>
      <p class="page-note">{CADENCE_LINE}</p>
    </header>

    <SubscribeForm />

    <div class="prose subscribe-page-note">
      <p>
        One email an issue. What we do with the address is set out in the{' '}
        <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reading us by machine instead? The <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a> and{' '}
        <a href="/feed.json">JSON</a> feeds carry every piece, and{' '}
        <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> maps the site.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The note sits under the field rather than above it, so nothing stands
     between a reader who arrived to subscribe and the field they came for. */
  .subscribe-page-note {
    max-width: 30rem;
    margin: 2.6rem auto 0;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .subscribe-page-note p + p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
</style>
